#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$47\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485841} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0c7edc-dd85-5992-a102-fb9214299a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485952} {:answer/id #uuid "da50aece-8c74-5d03-baea-3fa39526eb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$56\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486160} {:answer/id #uuid "e16e1058-e495-57a8-9aff-1460d83a15f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$48\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486177} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdba2ac-27cc-530d-9112-686fd6e96177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$60\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$47\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485841} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbdf95f2-cd1f-5ed7-bee1-5be1370106ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0668c811-3c2b-52a1-8452-9d77f52aa1d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335473" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve is monitoring the population of a newly introduced species of deer. The habitat can sustain a maximum population of $850$ deer, but the herd is still below this limit. Since biologists began tracking the growth, the population reached $490$ deer on month $4$ and $600$ deer on month $12.$ On what month will the population reach $800$ deer? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of deer, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in months.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the farm, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=850$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(850-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{850-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{850-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|850-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $850-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}850-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 850-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 850-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =850-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on month $4,$ the population reached $490$ deer. So, applying the condition $P(4)=490,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =490 \\\\ 850-K{e}^{-4k} & =490 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =360.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on month $12,$ the population was $600$ deer. So, applying the condition $P(12)=600,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =600 \\\\ 850-K{e}^{-12k} & =600 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{360}{250} \\\\ {e}^{8k} & =\\frac{36}{25} \\\\ 8k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{36}{25}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{36}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)} & =360 \\\\ K & =360{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)} \\\\ K & =360\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{25}} \\\\ K & =360\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ K & =432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=850-432{e}^{-(t/8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)}\n$$\n\nFinally, let $m$ be the month when the population reaches $800$ deer. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(m) & =800 \\\\ 850-432{e}^{-km} & =800 \\\\ 50 & =432{e}^{-km} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{50}{432} \\\\ -km & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{216}) \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{216}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $500$ deer on month\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{216})}{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{36}{25})}=47.309…≈47,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402149} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} {:answer/id #uuid "3c36fd19-5696-5a1e-9dc9-b04f43bf5c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402151} {:answer/id #uuid "31324eeb-37b1-5843-9dfa-024be7517c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402152} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8a292-e74d-5f79-93dd-d159881ed802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} :answer-field/id #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4a4a2d-af4a-591d-a8c6-0601fcd81ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485843} {:answer/id #uuid "6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485932} {:answer/id #uuid "74c4a600-f1cd-550e-8301-06f89a755629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485965} {:answer/id #uuid "9e717efe-369e-59b8-9552-86a39b1b2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$22\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486048} {:answer/id #uuid "e38477fa-d2cc-596e-abba-962f5089409f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17\\text{th}$ month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485932} :answer-field/id #uuid "9499f8ab-b442-53e2-8454-60e3c76d0bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "745cf184-0f4c-5376-bafe-668674640edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335649" :question/problem "A mountain valley is monitoring the population of a reintroduced elk herd. The valley can sustain a maximum population of $600$ elk, but the herd is still below this limit. Since ecologists began tracking the growth, the population reached $536$ elk on month $4$ and $573$ elk on month $10.$ On what month will the population reach $590$ elk? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of elk, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in months.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the valley can sustain, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ elk, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(600-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{600-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{600-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on month $4,$ the population was $536$ elk. So, applying the condition $P(4)=536,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =536 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =536 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on month $10,$ the population was $573$ elk. So, applying the condition $P(10)=573,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =573 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-10k} & =573 \\\\ K{e}^{-10k} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-10k}} & =\\frac{64}{27} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{64}{27} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{64}{27}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{64}{27})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} & =64 \\\\ K & =64{e}^{(4/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} \\\\ K & =64{e}^{(2/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} \\\\ K & =64{(\\frac{64}{27})}^{2/3} \\\\ K & =\\frac{1\\,024}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=600-\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $m$ be the month when the population reaches $590$ elk. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(m) & =590 \\\\ 600-\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-km} & =590 \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-km} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{90}{1\\,024} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{45}{512} \\\\ -km & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{45}{512}) \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{45}{512}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $590$ elk on month\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{45}{512})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{64}{27})}=16.905…≈17,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04278896-0d0b-5abb-8627-033b3e542c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75+H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485774} {:answer/id #uuid "09aa1eb1-e4d1-5ba6-831f-ff5b8460e95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k{H}^{2}(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485785} {:answer/id #uuid "37ece5ef-defd-5270-8c15-ef93957859b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485872} {:answer/id #uuid "5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485914} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0c4047-f0a5-5bbe-9e6d-196692c6e874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485914} :answer-field/id #uuid "15815c67-c7dd-5da5-a708-0978463c7ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207" :question/math-academy-id "q-344215" :question/problem "A hospital administrator observed that the growth of the number of occupied beds in a hospital is directly proportional to the difference between the hospital’s maximum bed capacity and the current number of occupied beds. Let $H(t)$ be the number of occupied beds, measured in beds, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of beds the hospital can support is $75$ beds, which differential equation could be used to model the number of occupied beds in the hospital? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of occupied beds $H(t)$ in the hospital changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of beds the hospital can support and the current number of occupied beds. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-H),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of beds the hospital can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=75\\text{ beds},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11749536-a0f7-5d13-b256-6fdeb5b98399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(320-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315223} {:answer/id #uuid "151ae25d-d64b-5851-adfb-806b30416fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(800-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315244} {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} {:answer/id #uuid "66e6db5c-1814-50aa-8ce2-db2edeec9f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315692} {:answer/id #uuid "c799b39e-df7d-5d1d-bea3-881572cd85f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} :answer-field/id #uuid "393ad745-9bcc-5868-a810-b633bf129cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3974627f-3801-52ea-b93f-3d1a3033e2cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-344233" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $320$ computers. The number of computers in the data center $C(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the data center is $1000$ computers, which differential equation could be used to model the number of computers? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of computers in the data center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of computers changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of computers in the data center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the data center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1000$ computers, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(1000-C).\n$$"}]}
